{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Chicago Cubs entered the season with championship expectations, but the opening days of March and early April delivered a jarring wake-up call. Carl Edwards Jr. surrendered a three-run homer on Opening Day, and the very next game saw Cole Hamels and Mike Montgomery get torched for a grand slam and a two-run triple in the same contest. The bullpen was leaking from the start, and a brutal series against Atlanta in early April compounded the damage. Yet the Cubs steadied themselves through April, finishing 14-10 and giving cautious optimism to the Wrigley faithful.\n\nMay and June told the story of a team capable of breathtaking highs and maddening lows in the same week. Javier Báez delivered a three-run walk-off blast in late April, and then May brought a wave of heroics — Willson Contreras, Kris Bryant, Jason Heyward, and Daniel Descalso all authored signature moments. But the bullpen kept undermining every surge. Steve Cishek coughed up a devastating two-run homer to Eugenio Suarez in late May, and Brandon Kintzler's meltdown against Philadelphia cost the Cubs dearly. June's Báez grand-slam moment stood as the high-water mark of the first half, but a 14-15 June record revealed a team spinning its wheels rather than pulling away.\n\nThe July and August stretch defined the season's ultimate fate. Craig Kimbrel, the expensive late-signing closer, proved wildly unreliable, surrendering a Christian Yelich home run in a critical late-July loss and then imploding again in September against the Cardinals. Derek Holland's catastrophic grand slam allowed to Bryce Harper in mid-August encapsulated a bullpen that could never be trusted in the biggest moments. Meanwhile, Nicholas Castellanos, acquired at the trade deadline, helped Bryant ignite a brief August rally that briefly rekindled playoff hopes, with Schwarber eclipsing 30 home runs to pace a lineup that scored 814 runs on the year.\n\nIn the end, the Cubs finished 84-78 — a winning record, but not enough. A September collapse at 11-16 buried any lingering postseason dreams. The relief corps, which had been a source of strength in the championship years, became the team's fatal flaw in 2019. Kimbrel's late arrival and shaky performance, the inconsistency of Strop and Cishek, and a rotation that couldn't always protect leads left Chicago watching October baseball from home for the first time since 2014. It was a season of tantalizing offensive firepower — 256 home runs, an .783 OPS — undermined at every turn by a bullpen that never found its footing.",
